    28	            value_type = TypeVarValue[T]()
    29	
    30	    Reading the attribute on a specialization yields the resolved type, and the
    31	    static type is narrowed to ``type[T]``::
    32	
    33	        Box[int].value_type        # int, statically type[int]
    34	        Box[str].value_type        # str, statically type[str]
    35	        Box[int]().value_type      # int (works on instances too)
    36	
    37	    The descriptor learns its *base* (the class it is defined on) once, in
    38	    ``__set_name__``. Resolution always happens against whatever class or
    39	    specialization you reach the attribute *through* — so the static type
    40	    (derived from that same receiver) cannot disagree with the runtime answer.
    41	    There is no ``cls2`` argument to wire wrong.
    42	
    43	    Inheritance works without ceremony: ``class Child[U](Box[U])`` then
    44	    ``Child[int].value_type`` resolves to ``int``, and a nested specialization
    45	    like ``class Arch[T](ArchBase[Wrap[T]])`` will resolve ``ArchBase``'s
    46	    typevar to ``Wrap[<arg>]`` when the descriptor is defined on ``ArchBase``.
